00:18:38,893 --> 00:18:39,614
[SPEAKER_03]: Sunny Lezda.

194
00:18:40,115 --> 00:18:40,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

195
00:18:41,016 --> 00:18:41,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Slightly less.

196
00:18:41,736 --> 00:18:45,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are still... Well, you don't want to personify it, because it's not a full person.

197
00:18:46,239 --> 00:18:47,380
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point.

198
00:18:47,460 --> 00:18:48,080
[SPEAKER_01]: It's important.

199
00:18:48,361 --> 00:18:48,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

200
00:18:49,181 --> 00:18:51,803
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to just think, oh, it's just another person, basically.

201
00:18:52,423 --> 00:18:53,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it isn't.

202
00:18:53,884 --> 00:18:54,345
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

203
00:18:54,405 --> 00:19:00,589
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, a young child, it's like, as he was saying, it's... it's... it's... the mind is still malleable.

204
00:19:02,110 --> 00:19:03,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like a full personality.

205
00:19:04,091 --> 00:19:04,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the animal.

206
00:19:04,952 --> 00:19:06,033
[SPEAKER_01]: You could personify it.

207
00:19:06,433 --> 00:19:07,634
[SPEAKER_01]: You could give it more than it is.

208
00:19:08,194 --> 00:19:08,975
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is,

209
00:19:10,247 --> 00:19:14,008
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's another person, it kind of becomes like a complete thing.

210
00:19:14,828 --> 00:19:20,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the way people relate with each other, it's like, well, I'm I, you're you, you do your thing, I do my thing.

211
00:19:20,830 --> 00:19:21,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

212
00:19:21,451 --> 00:19:24,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, my mind has its own mind, it does its own, I'll do my own thing.

213
00:19:24,592 --> 00:19:25,572
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, no, no, no.

214
00:19:26,252 --> 00:19:28,313
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's the child or the animal.

215
00:19:28,393 --> 00:19:34,295
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... you can't have that degree of independence as you would from another person.

216
00:19:34,315 --> 00:19:34,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

217
00:19:35,752 --> 00:19:40,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why I was saying before, in the previous talk, it's important to not overly personify it.

218
00:19:41,236 --> 00:19:50,264
[SPEAKER_01]: On the other hand, you don't want to under-personify it either, saying, oh, it's just chemical brain processes in response to stimuli and has no consciousness of its own.

219
00:19:50,324 --> 00:19:54,407
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, it does, because it makes its own choices, obviously, that you're pressured by.

220
00:19:55,528 --> 00:19:59,171
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, there is that degree of autonomy of citta.

221
00:20:01,483 --> 00:20:03,344
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you add too much to it, you won't see it.

222
00:20:03,624 --> 00:20:06,305
[SPEAKER_01]: If you take away too much from it, you won't see it either.

223
00:20:14,968 --> 00:20:19,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, the goal is to tame the child, or teach it rightly, or tame the animal.

224
00:20:20,670 --> 00:20:25,392
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're overly emotionally invested, you will not... you will lose perspective.

225
00:20:26,052 --> 00:20:28,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if you just say, well, I wash my hands of you,

226
00:20:29,246 --> 00:20:30,047
[SPEAKER_01]: You do your own thing.

227
00:20:30,087 --> 00:20:38,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you might feel freer for that period of time, but factually, the goal of training that child is not complete.

228
00:20:38,272 --> 00:20:39,693
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's the opposite.

229
00:20:39,773 --> 00:20:43,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't really just, just, okay, well, I'll just do my thing.

230
00:20:43,156 --> 00:20:43,856
[SPEAKER_01]: You go on your own.

231
00:20:44,617 --> 00:20:45,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Not with your citta.

232
00:20:45,437 --> 00:20:46,518
[SPEAKER_01]: You can do that with other people.

233
00:20:47,299 --> 00:20:53,143
[SPEAKER_01]: But with your citta, no, you're just going to stay where you are, and the result of that attitude will be you're not aiming it.

234
00:20:55,645 --> 00:20:58,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it where, like, the kind of ascetic practice come from, like, this kind of

235
00:20:59,590 --> 00:21:02,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Wanting to push citta away and...

236
00:21:02,211 --> 00:21:03,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much, yeah, yeah.

237
00:21:03,392 --> 00:21:07,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Have a clean cut through all of this, so it doesn't even have to come up, so I don't have to deal with it.

238
00:21:07,895 --> 00:21:08,975
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what it is.

239
00:21:09,595 --> 00:21:13,317
[SPEAKER_01]: The... those austere self-mortification practices.

240
00:21:13,738 --> 00:21:18,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, doing such practices in relation to citta can be beneficial.

241
00:21:19,361 --> 00:21:24,123
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why Buddha said, well, let those who want to do those austere practices, do them.

242
00:21:25,264 --> 00:21:28,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Not make them mandatory, because it will actually result in, oh, okay,

243
00:21:29,979 --> 00:21:30,700
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll stick to this.

244
00:21:31,300 --> 00:21:32,441
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't see my mind anymore.

245
00:21:32,561 --> 00:21:33,522
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not my problem.

246
00:21:33,582 --> 00:21:34,683
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, it remains your problem.

247
00:21:34,824 --> 00:21:35,865
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just ignoring it now.

248
00:21:43,332 --> 00:21:48,777
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes it will be even sort of counterintuitive even to your own values.

249
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:52,540
[SPEAKER_05]: Very much so.

250
00:21:53,361 --> 00:21:54,302
[SPEAKER_01]: When it comes to, like,

251
00:21:57,317 --> 00:22:06,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Clarifying and outlining mind rightly, as I said, you shouldn't be overly emotionally invested, but you shouldn't be trying to get rid of it or sort of dismiss it either.

252
00:22:07,525 --> 00:22:11,388
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes, in order to do neither of those things, you realize, oh, so what?

253
00:22:11,428 --> 00:22:15,111
[SPEAKER_01]: I just have to eat milk porridge now, like a householder.

254
00:22:15,831 --> 00:22:16,792
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not right.

255
00:22:17,813 --> 00:22:21,616
[SPEAKER_01]: By any ascetic, by any self-respecting sort of summoner, that's not right.

256
00:22:24,027 --> 00:22:26,429
[SPEAKER_01]: But then the Buddha realized, well, actually, it's not about that.

257
00:22:26,569 --> 00:22:28,430
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about where my values are currently.

258
00:22:29,411 --> 00:22:33,534
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about direct input from the mind, from the citta.

259
00:22:33,674 --> 00:22:38,518
[SPEAKER_01]: How does this... And then he realized, oh, if I don't do this, I will not be able to train it.

260
00:22:42,401 --> 00:22:45,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind is too weak.

261
00:22:45,663 --> 00:22:46,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Too invisible, basically.

262
00:22:48,085 --> 00:22:50,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Too beaten down by everything else he was doing.

263
00:22:52,788 --> 00:23:12,142
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that's, again, that's what's saying, like, and even in the suttas, the Buddha would often, when he came to these ascetic practices, and how much, and where is the line, he said, well, you realize the line, you have the broad line, things never to be done, obvious, the eight precepts, and so on, but then all this other was, like, he was dependent on where the mind was.

264
00:23:13,343 --> 00:23:18,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes eating milk, rice, will obscure your mind, depending on where your intention was.

265
00:23:18,767 --> 00:23:19,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it will reveal it.

266
00:23:20,605 --> 00:23:22,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Depending on where your intention to not eat it was.

267
00:23:25,846 --> 00:23:27,107
[SPEAKER_01]: But for that, you need to see the mind.

268
00:23:27,227 --> 00:23:28,027
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the reference.

269
00:23:29,087 --> 00:23:31,908
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you have that reference, then you can train happily day and night.

270
00:23:32,748 --> 00:23:33,428
[SPEAKER_01]: As they say.

271
00:23:33,448 --> 00:23:35,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you realize, oh, this is the input.

272
00:23:35,549 --> 00:23:36,989
[SPEAKER_01]: No external.

273
00:23:37,029 --> 00:23:37,609
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you do that?

274
00:23:37,629 --> 00:23:38,169
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not right.

275
00:23:38,189 --> 00:23:39,070
[SPEAKER_01]: You shouldn't be doing that.

276
00:23:39,670 --> 00:23:40,690
[SPEAKER_01]: You shouldn't be eating rice.

277
00:23:42,010 --> 00:23:45,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, and that's exactly what his former friends and companions, they were disgusted with him.

278
00:23:46,331 --> 00:23:47,632
[SPEAKER_01]: He reverted to luxury.

279
00:23:48,412 --> 00:23:49,012
[SPEAKER_01]: And they left him.

280
00:23:50,348 --> 00:23:55,731
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if he was caring about that, about his peers approving of him, he would have never become the Buddha.

281
00:23:56,791 --> 00:24:02,674
[SPEAKER_01]: If he was caring about the tradition that he's been following, established culture, he would have never become the Buddha.

282
00:24:02,875 --> 00:24:05,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Because none of that has a mind as a reference point.

283
00:24:06,476 --> 00:24:11,159
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just circumstantial involvement of everything and nothing, really.

284
00:24:17,402 --> 00:24:18,923
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep saying, like, you know,

285
00:24:20,394 --> 00:24:21,755
[SPEAKER_01]: The framework is the eight precepts.

286
00:24:23,616 --> 00:24:34,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything within that, if it's tighter or looser, well, that should be determined individually, every single time, on the basis of where your mind is and why you're doing it for.

287
00:24:39,565 --> 00:24:42,667
[SPEAKER_05]: I think, yeah, it's good to emphasize as well, like, every single time.

288
00:24:42,747 --> 00:24:48,130
[SPEAKER_05]: Because even, let's say, milk, rice, the same thing, it's very so easy to say, oh,

289
00:24:48,952 --> 00:24:51,074
[SPEAKER_05]: I find I always want this particular thing.

290
00:24:51,395 --> 00:24:52,856
[SPEAKER_05]: I'll just give it up.

291
00:24:52,876 --> 00:24:53,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

292
00:24:54,077 --> 00:25:01,585
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's much harder to notice that on some days, it can be rooted in craving and pressure.

293
00:25:01,705 --> 00:25:02,526
[SPEAKER_05]: Then you don't do it.

294
00:25:03,287 --> 00:25:05,229
[SPEAKER_05]: And on another occasion, or actually, it's not.

295
00:25:05,730 --> 00:25:05,830
[SPEAKER_05]: And

296
00:25:06,630 --> 00:25:08,751
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, you should actually allow it.

297
00:25:08,771 --> 00:25:09,031
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

298
00:25:09,051 --> 00:25:10,012
[SPEAKER_01]: But I still won't do it.

299
00:25:10,552 --> 00:25:13,894
[SPEAKER_01]: And people would fall into that trap, like, yeah, but it doesn't matter, thus I won't do it.

300
00:25:14,635 --> 00:25:15,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it doesn't really matter.

301
00:25:15,795 --> 00:25:18,437
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, well, it's true, but see, you could say the same in reverse.

302
00:25:19,297 --> 00:25:20,998
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't really matter, so why don't you do it?

303
00:25:22,939 --> 00:25:24,320
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't really matter, so I won't do it.

304
00:25:25,801 --> 00:25:34,266
[SPEAKER_01]: So it just becomes a justification to just keep tightening, and tightening, and tightening, out of some, as I said, perceived sense of duty.

305
00:25:34,426 --> 00:25:36,107
[SPEAKER_05]: It forces you to, like,

306
00:25:37,768 --> 00:25:39,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that forces you to raise your child properly.

