00:25:41,610 --> 00:25:49,273
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just, oh, he was fine eating this yesterday, I can just give it to it today without having to look at it or think about it.

308
00:25:49,673 --> 00:25:50,814
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what it becomes.

309
00:25:51,334 --> 00:25:59,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, he wasn't fine eating yesterday, thus I will keep forbidding it because, again, it's easier to just adhere to the view now that came out of it.

310
00:26:01,177 --> 00:26:05,739
[SPEAKER_01]: You could reasonably recognize every single time where intentions are rooted.

311
00:26:06,420 --> 00:26:08,101
[SPEAKER_01]: What state of the child is it?

312
00:26:08,681 --> 00:26:14,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it unruly, crying, screaming, having a fit to get what he wants?

313
00:26:14,584 --> 00:26:14,784
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

314
00:26:17,005 --> 00:26:23,048
[SPEAKER_01]: If it isn't, and now it's maybe even a bit depressed or something, okay, give it now to loosen up a bit.

315
00:26:23,857 --> 00:26:29,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Within, as I said, what was defined as the eight precepts.

316
00:26:29,479 --> 00:26:31,059
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't go past that line, that's too much.

317
00:26:33,620 --> 00:26:35,900
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a constant work, in that sense.

318
00:26:36,341 --> 00:26:38,021
[SPEAKER_01]: You constantly have to keep an eye on it, then.

319
00:26:38,641 --> 00:26:46,143
[SPEAKER_01]: You constantly have to be checking in and monitoring which way it takes, and that's just too cumbersome.

320
00:26:47,404 --> 00:26:51,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Because at the same time, I have this view offered now, that if I just keep

321
00:26:52,376 --> 00:26:53,898
[SPEAKER_01]: This has a rule to it.

322
00:26:55,380 --> 00:26:58,964
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be the same effect, minus me having to deal with it.

323
00:26:59,765 --> 00:27:01,747
[SPEAKER_01]: And the point is, it won't have the same effect.

324
00:27:02,828 --> 00:27:03,509
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not learning.

325
00:27:04,150 --> 00:27:05,311
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not learning, exactly.

326
00:27:05,631 --> 00:27:06,252
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not learning.

327
00:27:07,934 --> 00:27:10,137
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the the simile of the of the deer herd.

328
00:27:10,217 --> 00:27:11,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when

329
00:27:12,403 --> 00:27:22,306
[SPEAKER_01]: When the group of ascetic recognized, oh, yeah, this is all the bait and Mara's snare, so they shun the bait, go on the mountain, say no to everything, and then they begin to starve, basically.

330
00:27:23,046 --> 00:27:27,328
[SPEAKER_01]: And when they do return to stuff to eat, they still don't distinguish where the bait is.

331
00:27:27,748 --> 00:27:29,988
[SPEAKER_01]: So they get, again, trapped by Mara.

332
00:27:31,169 --> 00:27:36,630
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the final deer herd was the one that learned how to eat around the bait.

333
00:27:37,311 --> 00:27:38,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Every single time.

334
00:27:40,112 --> 00:27:41,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to eat it unwarily.

335
00:27:43,055 --> 00:27:43,795
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly it.

336
00:27:43,835 --> 00:27:53,938
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's like, every single time, the reference point should be the state of your citta, for whether to act or not act, whether it's beneficial, whether it's unbeneficial.

337
00:27:55,678 --> 00:28:00,919
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole training in a nutshell, really.

338
00:28:01,560 --> 00:28:03,220
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole practice of jhāna, it's exactly that.

339
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:10,782
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just taming the citta rightly, not choking it and asphyxiating it, and not letting it run loose.

340
00:28:12,457 --> 00:28:18,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Until it's basically stabilized on the safe ground and it recognizes that.

341
00:28:19,519 --> 00:28:21,420
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... the result is citta viveka.

342
00:28:21,640 --> 00:28:23,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Viveka from the unwholesome states.

343
00:28:24,401 --> 00:28:28,042
[SPEAKER_01]: From the pulls, from the pressures, from the hindrances, from the obstructions.

344
00:28:30,343 --> 00:28:33,804
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the Buddha says in the Sutta, go monks and practice jhāna, that's what he means.

345
00:28:33,864 --> 00:28:37,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean go monks and do some meditation technique.

346
00:28:38,267 --> 00:28:40,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it would be at the end of such suttas.

347
00:28:40,809 --> 00:28:51,535
[SPEAKER_01]: See, they would talk about the sutta about purifying your mind, not giving in to unwholesome states, instruct the monks in all these details, and then tell them, okay, now you heard this, now go and do the jhāna.

348
00:28:52,916 --> 00:28:57,118
[SPEAKER_01]: If jhāna was something completely different from that, he wouldn't have said it at the end of that context.

349
00:28:59,920 --> 00:29:07,765
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, at the end of different contexts of types of purification and how to attend to things, he would address going now and do the jhāna.

350
00:29:08,990 --> 00:29:14,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Do the jhāna basically means do this mind training on account of this context I'm providing you.

351
00:29:14,694 --> 00:29:17,716
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it was unique to him, unique to the Buddhist practice.

352
00:29:21,219 --> 00:29:35,370
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where exactly the results in overcoming the five hindrances, when the unruly child becomes fully disciplined, understanding, seeing for itself what's good for it.

353
00:29:36,269 --> 00:29:45,513
[SPEAKER_01]: When the wild animal is fully tamed, obedient, pretty much knows what you think, without even having to do it, to say it, to tell it to do.

354
00:29:49,315 --> 00:29:50,656
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you both live happily.

355
00:29:50,956 --> 00:29:52,577
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the pleasant abiding here and now.

356
00:29:53,985 --> 00:30:09,358
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only thing that can disturb that, is if you start carelessly going back to sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, like this, so to say, that then, indirectly, start disturbing the animal again, because it wasn't completely... well, it wasn't completely freed.

357
00:30:10,899 --> 00:30:20,968
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to calm it, withdraw it from the unwholesome, stabilize it on the elevated, safe ground, the island, but then protect it there.

358
00:30:22,011 --> 00:30:22,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't think now.

359
00:30:22,772 --> 00:30:25,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you will think, oh, I can relax now.

360
00:30:25,414 --> 00:30:26,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's true, you can relax.

361
00:30:27,276 --> 00:30:28,497
[SPEAKER_01]: But not in regard to the bait.

362
00:30:28,697 --> 00:30:29,958
[SPEAKER_01]: You can relax in regard to the animal.

363
00:30:30,438 --> 00:30:31,779
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't forget the bait is still there.

364
00:30:33,301 --> 00:30:39,226
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although you can probably handle it more than you used to, you are still grabbing onto the hooks and traps unnecessarily.

365
00:30:39,666 --> 00:30:41,007
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is agitating the animal.

366
00:30:41,288 --> 00:30:44,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe not as much as it used to, but it's still agitating it.

367
00:30:45,992 --> 00:30:47,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's absolutely no reason to do it.

368
00:30:50,651 --> 00:30:52,832
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's the priority thing.

369
00:30:57,354 --> 00:31:02,636
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the thoroughbred will never be alone from the horse trainer.

370
00:31:04,536 --> 00:31:05,997
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't get rid of the horse trainer.

371
00:31:07,638 --> 00:31:16,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Initially, he might think or assume that the safety would be... the greatest safety would be if there is no horse trainer.

372
00:31:17,634 --> 00:31:21,717
[SPEAKER_01]: He has to come to terms to accept that he'll never be alone from the horse trainer.

373
00:31:22,558 --> 00:31:29,002
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way out from the whip of the horse trainer is to tame his behavior, not remove the horse trainer.

374
00:31:31,624 --> 00:31:38,149
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's... and same like in the reverse order simile we gave, it's like the animal is watching you.

375
00:31:38,830 --> 00:31:41,952
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is basically seeing only what you do.

376
00:31:42,997 --> 00:31:45,282
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, you're never wrong from it.

377
00:31:45,422 --> 00:31:47,727
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you can never be yourself fully.

378
00:31:51,315 --> 00:31:53,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're always undermined by the other.

